646,"_gp_rev_volatility":false,"_score_rev_volatility":51.9,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":0.09769861568450283,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":false,"_score_margin_vs_hist":0.0,"_gate_piotroski":5,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":55.6,"_gate_spread":null,"_gp_spread":null,"_score_spread":null,"_gate_roic_consistency":null,"_gp_roic_consistency":null,"_score_roic_consistency":null,"_gate_incr_roic":null,"_gp_incr_roic":null,"_score_incr_roic":null,"_gate_margin_advantage":null,"_gp_margin_advantage":null,"_score_margin_advantage":null,"_gate_pool_share":0.06781568807447913,"_gp_pool_share":true,"_score_pool_share":67.1,"_gate_rev_durability":0.18669258224812824,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":100.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":null,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.029497346652906247,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":43.9,"_gate_share_shrink":0.001820068470299585,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":47.7,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.012977146993895737,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":78.4,"_gate_insider_own":0.03951,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":26.3,"_gate_insider_buying":1.0,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":100.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Primis Financial Corp. operates as the bank holding company for Primis Bank that provides various financial services to individuals, and small and medium sized businesses in the United States. The company offers deposit products, including checking, NOW, savings, and money market accounts, as well as certificates of deposits; and commercial deposit products comprising investment/sweep accounts, wire transfer services, employer services/payroll processing services, zero balance accounts, night depository services, depository transfers, merchant services, ACH originations, business debit cards, controlled disbursement accounts, and remote deposit capture services. It also provides commercial lending products, such as loans consist of lines of credit, revolving credit facilities, demand loans, term loans, equipment loans, SBA loans, stand-by letters of credit, and unsecured loans; loans for permanent financing; construction loans for commercial, multi-family, assisted living and other non-residential properties, and builder/developer lines; second asset based lending; SBA lending; financing for medical, dental, and veterinary businesses; and warehouse lending lines of credit to residential mortgage originators. In addition, the company provides consumer lending products comprising residential mortgage, home equity lines of credit, secured and unsecured personal loans, life insurance premium financing, and Panacea consumer loans. Further, it offers debit cards, ATM services, notary services, and mobile and online banking. The company was formerly known as Southern National Bancorp of Virginia, Inc. and changed its name to Primis Financial Corp. Primis Financial Corp. was founded in 2004 and is headquartered in McLean, Virginia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Dennis J. Zember Jr. | President, CEO & Director | Age 55 | Compensation: $1.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 593 people. Revenue per employee of $377K ranks in the below the sector median among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 16%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $15K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Primis Financial Corp. is a small-cap financial services business with a $362M market cap on $224M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +21% over the past three years and +19% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 6% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $9M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 4 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders. Cash conversion is thin at 0.18\u00d7, however, which raises the oldest question in analysis: are these earnings backed by cash?.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 0.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.9% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 4.0% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.2) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 53% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Insiders are net buyers (100% buy ratio) \u2014 insiders sell for many reasons but buy for only one","14 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Growing faster than most Financial Services peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"(FRST) as a Liquidity Pulse for Institutional Tactics","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/101/FRST_as_a_Liquidity_Pulse_for_Institutional_Tactics_111126093001_1794407401.html","date":"2026-11-11","timestamp":1794407401.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"How Primis Financial Corp. (FRST) Affects Rotational Strategy Timing","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/139/How_Primis_Financial_Corp._FRST_Affects_Rotational_Strategy_Timing_110126093201_1793543521.html","date":"2026-11-01","timestamp":1793543521.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Primis Financial declares $0.10 dividend (NASDAQ:FRST)","source":"seekingalpha.com","link":"https://seekingalpha.com/news/4657183-primis-financial-declares-0_10-dividend","date":"2026-10-26","timestamp":1793022839.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["dividend"]},{"title":"Do Options Traders Know Something About FRST Stock We Don't?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3014833/do-options-traders-know-something-about-frst-stock-we-don-t?cid=CS-ZC-FT-tale_of_the_tape%7Coptions-3014833","date":"2026-10-26","timestamp":1793015160.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope